int: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186060051 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8\\,\\text{oz},\\,n=100,\\,\\sigma =2\\,\\text{oz}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.392\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186060053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186060053 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n = 180$ from a population with standard deviation $σ = 2$. Given that the mean of the sample is $x^(―) =-5$, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 2.576) = 0.005$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186060053 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=180$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186060053 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ -5\\pm 0.384\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499462 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499476 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499534 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064297 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499538 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499561 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499451 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499537 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186064315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499566 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067721 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067722 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067723 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067724 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067725 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498885 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498914 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498964 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498991 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186067720 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499029 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186