ref 17592186499529 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499554 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186124456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499527 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499518 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186124652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499521 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186129197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499479 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186129262 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499490 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186135494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498828 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186135494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498828 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138166 1071 "The force $F$, measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a$, measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$. When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$, the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$. What is the constant of variation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138166 1071 "The force $F,$ measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2.$ When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ What is the constant of variation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138166 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is\n\n$$\na=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2\n$$\n\nthe force is\n\n$$\nF=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2000 &= k \\cdot (5) \\\\\n2000 &= 5k \\\\\n\\frac{2000}{5} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{F}{a}\n$$\n\nwe have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{kg m/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} &= \\frac{kgm/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} \\\\\n&= kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nk = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138166 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is $a=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,000 & =k\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\,000 & =5k \\\\ \\frac{2\\,000}{5} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = \\dfrac{F}{a},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{kg m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cancel{{\\text{m/s}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138168 1071 "The stretch $s$, measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$, of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l$, measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g})$. When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g}$, the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138168 1071 "The stretch $s,$ measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l,$ measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g}).$ When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138168 1074 "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l$, so we have\n\n$s = k{l}$,\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when\n\n$$\nl=8\\,\\mathrm{g}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\ns=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9.6 &= k \\cdot (8) \\\\\n9.6 &= 8k \\\\\n\\frac{9.6}{8} &= k \\\\\n1.2 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nl=6 \\,\\textrm{g}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138168 1074 "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\ns = k{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ we have $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9.6 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 9.6 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{9.6}{8} & =k \\\\ 1.2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ is $l=6 \\,\\textrm{g}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138171 1071 "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L$, measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m$, then what equation models this situation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138171 1071 "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L,$ measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m,$ then what equation models this situation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138171 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = kL^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=4\\,\\textrm{N}$ when $L=0.1\\,\\textrm m.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =k\\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =k\\cdot 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{4}{0.01} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nF = 400L^2.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138171 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n$F = kL^2$,\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nF=4\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nL=0.1\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= k \\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\\n4 &= k \\cdot 0.01 \\\\\n\\frac{4}{0.01} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n$F = 400L^2$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499522 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499543 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499547 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499473 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499532 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138600 1050 600.0 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138738 1071 "The volume of space $V$ a particular gas occupies, measured in liters $(L)$, is directly proportional to its temperature $T$, measured in Kelvin $(K)$. The volume of the gas is $V = 48L$ when its temperature is $T = 360K$. Find the volume of the gas when its temperature is $300K$.The volume of the gas is\n{{field-1}} $L$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186138738 1071 "The volume of space $V$ a particular gas occupies, measured in liters $(L),$ is directly proportional to its temperature $T,$ measured in Kelvin $(\\text{K}).$ The volume of the gas is $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ when its temperature is $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ Find the volume of the gas when its temperature is $300\\,\\text{K}.$\n\nThe volume of the gas is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{L}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186138738 1074 "The volume of the gas can be modeled as a direct variation\n\n$$\nV=kT,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that the gas occupies $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ at $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =k\\cdot 360 \\\\ \\frac{48}{360} & =k \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}T.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the volume occupied by the gas when $T=300\\,\\text{K}$:\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot 300=40\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the temperature of the gas is $300\\,\\text{K},$ its volume is $40\\,\\text{L}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186139717 1064 "A good example of a direct variation model is the distance an object travels when moving at a constant rate. In this case, the corresponding direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nd=vt\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $d$ is the total distance traveled by the object,\n- $t$ is the total time that the object has been traveling, and,\n- $v$ is the constant of proportionality, called the **speed** of the object.\n\nFor instance, if an object travels a distance of $24\\,\\textrm m$ in $4$ seconds, then we can substitute\n\n$$\nd=24\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nt=4\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\ninto the above equation and solve for $v$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 &= v \\cdot 4 \\\\\n\\frac{24}{4} &= v \\\\\n6 &= v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation that relates $d$ and $t$ is\n$d=6t$.\n\nOne thing to note is that the constant of proportionality $v = 6$ also has units. So let's work out what these units are.\n\nSince we have\n\n$d = vt$,\nit follows that\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{d}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, the units of $v$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm m}{\\textrm s} = \\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality (including its unit) is\n\n$$\nv = 6\\, \\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186139717 1064 "A good example of a direct variation model is the distance an object travels when moving at a constant rate. In this case, the corresponding direct variation equation is \n\n$$\nd=vt,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $d$ is the total distance traveled by the object,\n\n- $t$ is the total time that the object has been traveling, and,\n\n- $v$ is the constant of proportionality, called the **speed** of the object.\n\nFor instance, if an object travels a distance of $24\\,\\textrm m$ in $4$ seconds, then we can substitute $d=24\\,\\textrm m$ and $t=4\\,\\textrm s$ into the above equation and solve for $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =v\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & =v \\\\ 6 & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation that relates $d$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\nd=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nOne thing to note is that the constant of proportionality $v = 6$ also has units. So let's work out what these units are.\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\nd = vt,\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{d}{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the units of $v$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm m}{\\textrm s} = \\textrm m / \\textrm s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality (including its unit) is $v = 6\\, \\textrm m / \\textrm s.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186139719 1064 "When modeling proportional relationships, we sometimes find that a quantity $y$ varies directly with the *square* of a second quantity $x$. In such cases, we write\n\n$$\ny = kx^2\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a circle of radius $r$, measured in centimeters.\n\n![](images/fa/fad3151a754d96d3e4ad97fa812ce7087601cebe7c4ab00f7b3c8cd603b140c0.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the area $A$, measured in square meters, of a circle varies directly with the *square* of the radius $r$. Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\nA = k r^2\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186139719 1064 "When modeling proportional relationships, we sometimes find that a quantity $y$ varies directly with the *square* of a second quantity $x.$ In such cases, we write\n\n$$\ny = kx^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a circle of radius $r,$ measured in centimeters.\n\n![](images/fa/fad3151a754d96d3e4ad97fa812ce7087601cebe7c4ab00f7b3c8cd603b140c0.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the area $A,$ measured in square meters, of a circle varies directly with the *square* of the radius $r.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\nA = k r^2.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186179766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499455 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186179766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499535 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499510 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499564 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186179776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499504 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197625 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197626 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197627 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197628 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186197629 13194139535343 17592186045417 false] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498740 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186197624 1075 #edb/r