] [17592186499439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499468 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499506 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499540 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499439 1039 "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499439 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499498 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499439 1042 #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499480 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499525 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499541 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499550 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499551 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1039 "Finding a Value by Approximating Ratios" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499464 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499440 1042 #uuid "a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499448 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499499 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499526 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499531 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1039 "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Hundred" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499450 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499441 1042 #uuid "c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499365 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1069 "q-345269" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1072 #uuid "00663209-74b8-5c10-8a0a-2c782390f942" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499442 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3bbe131778b11fe61a7e436d3d4729958bb207961ca9faaac91250cf4f2dde0.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+9\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499385 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1069 "q-143023" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1071 "A primary school teacher records how many words his second-year students can read in 5 minutes. The number of words read is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =36$ words. In a school of $180$ students, he finds that the mean number of read words is $255$ words. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean number of words $\\mu $ for the entire population of second-year children.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1072 #uuid "094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499443 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=255,\\,n=180,\\,\\sigma =36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}255\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ 255\\pm 5.26\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499382 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499397 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1069 "q-555450" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1072 #uuid "0afb67b2-5531-5190-bc7f-fa3f3f29c54e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499444 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $57.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $57$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<57<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{57}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{57}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b5ce9c24d223674d04686a577347e43cd40728194596e75df7c0faa006d2888.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $7$ and $8.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=7$ and $b=8.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499339 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1069 "q-556442" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1072 #uuid "0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499445 1074 "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{220}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{35}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nSince $250>220,$ we know that $\\sqrt[3]{250}>\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ is the greatest number among the given options." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499367 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1069 "q-60014" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1071 "The following table shows the number of children in a park whose eyes are blue or brown.\n\n| Blue Eyes | Brown Eyes |\n| --- | --- |\n| $22$ | $35$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of the number of children with blue eyes to the number of children with brown eyes." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1072 #uuid "12682823-7d0b-5372-9b66-9cc9b42322aa" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499446 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of children with blue eyes and the number of children with brown eyes by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- number of children with blue eyes: $22⟶20$\n\n- number of children with brown eyes: $35⟶40$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of children with blue eyes to the number of children with brown eyes is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{40}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20÷10}{40÷10}=\\frac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of children with blue eyes to the number of children with brown eyes is approximately $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499362 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499363 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1069 "q-556446" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1072 #uuid "1a16d859-8fe1-5bbc-a36b-6b57bd9a46c0" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499447 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $50$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<50<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656 \\\\ {3.7}^{3} & =50.653 \\\\ {3.8}^{3} & =54.872\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $46.656<50<50.653$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.6}^{3}<50<{3.7}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.6<\\sqrt[3]{50}<3.7\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between $3.6$ and $3.7$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=3.6$ and $b=3.7$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499417 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1069 "q-59842" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1071 "An election took place in a small town between two political parties, The Green Party and The Labour Party. The table below shows the total number of votes that each party received.\n\n| The Green Party | The Labour Party |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,020$ | $3\\,022$ |\n\nBy rounding to the nearest hundred, find the approximate ratio of Labour Party votes to total votes." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1072 #uuid "1c338986-28f6-5449-8d51-483994f85450" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499448 1074 "First, let's approximate the total number of votes that each party received by rounding to the nearest hundred.\n\n- The Green Party: $1\\,020⟶1\\,000$\n\n- The Labour Party: $3\\,022⟶3\\,000$\n\nSo the total number of votes received was approximately $1\\,000+3\\,000=4\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of Labour Party votes to total votes (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\,000}{4\\,000}.\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $1\\,000$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\,000÷1\\,000}{4\\,000÷1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of Labour Party votes to total votes is approximately $\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499325 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1069 "q-161868" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1072 #uuid "21557ab9-79b3-55a0-a104-51c3d273599e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499449 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499450 1069 "e-2282" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499450 1071 "The following table shows the number of boys and girls in a school.\n\n| Boys | Girls |\n| --- | --- |\n| $441$ | $569$ |\n\nRounding the given values to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students (boys and girls)." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499450 1072 #uuid "23152915-ce5d-502c-9347-c4889b9103df" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499450 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of boys and girls by rounding to the nearest hundred.\n\n- Number of boys: $441 \\longrightarrow 400$\n\n- Number of girls: $569 \\longrightarrow 600$\n\nThus, the total number of students is approximately $400 + 600 = 1,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400}{1,000} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{400 \\div 100}{1,000 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of boys to the total number of students is approximately $\\dfrac{2}{5}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499389 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1069 "q-143499" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}$ be a random sample of size $n=40$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08,$ calculate $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8413\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8461\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8643\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8665\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8849\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8888\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1072 #uuid "23386dfb-4266-50f7-b8bd-add2ea98080d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499451 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.5,\\frac{0.08}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.5,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =P(\\frac{0.51-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{0.55-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.22<Z<1.12) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.12)-P(Z<0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.22)=0.5871, \\\\ Φ(1.12)=0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22) \\\\  & =0.8686-0.5871 \\\\  & =0.2815.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499406 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1069 "q-87769" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1071 "![](images/c1/c13c5ac52b7bc508032d2d53b1169730641bafefcd8c0f5487d96699d6120946.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $P$ and $Q$ shown above. Given that $WXY≅FGH,$ what is the perimeter of $Q?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1072 #uuid "25b1bc91-494d-53b8-abed-15f163bc2377" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499452 1074 "The statement $WXY≅FGH$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $G,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $H.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FG & =WX \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HF & =YW \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe figure shows that $FG=GH.$ Therefore, the perimeter of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{Q} & =FG+GH+HF \\\\  & =5+5+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =5(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499373 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1069 "q-556431" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1072 #uuid "283f891f-b99b-51a0-abad-92ec462820ac" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499453 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between, we can compare $20$ to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than $20$ is $8,$ since ${2}^{3}=8.$\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than $20$ is $27,$ since ${3}^{3}=27.$\n\nThis means $20$ is between $8$ and $27:$\n\n$$\n8<20<27\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{8} & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ 2 & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between $2$ and $3.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499352 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1069 "q-345414" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1071 "![](images/df/dfe6902183d348f21d053acce448bd5012d190b6b678f406099ef9d0a2c83374.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1072 #uuid "2ce9d0ba-72fa-5144-a2b0-16cd598fe603" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499454 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499306 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1069 "q-162638" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+4y)\\,i-(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y)\\,j$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1072 #uuid "310694c7-b1be-5cf8-b20f-09c0a8e64636" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499455 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4{x}^{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x-(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499313 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1069 "q-60009" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1071 "The following table shows the number of cakes and cookies sold on a charity day.\n\n| Cakes | Cookies |\n| --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $121$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the ratio of the number of cakes sold to the number of cookies sold." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1072 #uuid "36e19ee6-6d4d-5a04-b273-015238cdc5ad" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499456 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of cakes and cookies sold by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- number of cakes sold: $23⟶20$\n\n- number of cookies sold: $121⟶120$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of cakes sold to the number of cookies sold is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{120}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20÷10}{120÷10}=\\frac{2}{12}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2÷2}{12÷2}=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of cakes sold to the number of cookies sold is approximately $\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499457 1069 "e-2283" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499457 1071 "The ratio of the number of orchids to the number of roses in a garden is $1: 3.$ In the garden, there are $84$ orchids. Rounding the number of orchids to the nearest ten, approximate the number of roses in the garden." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499457 1072 #uuid "392a598d-d5a4-555b-96d5-3e6125a3b2b6" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499457 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of orchids:\n\n$$\n84 \\longrightarrow 80\n$$\n\nWe are told that ratio of the number of orchids to the number of roses in the garden is $1:3,$ which can be expressed as the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3} \\, .\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the number of orchids and the denominator represents the number of roses.\n\nWe know that there are approximately $80$ orchids, so we want to make a numerator of $80.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $80\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 \\times 80}{3 \\times 80} = \\dfrac{80}{240}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $240$ roses in the garden." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499391 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1069 "q-142495" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1071 "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=64$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1072 #uuid "3aeeb390-5562-50bf-80f7-9c922c7da785" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499458 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 8\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499334 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1069 "q-60057" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1071 "There are $121$ boys in Eastwood High School. The ratio of the number of boys to the number of girls is $3:5.$ By rounding the number of boys to the nearest ten, approximate the number of girls in Eastwood High School." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1072 #uuid "3ceac12d-489e-5694-8f52-b302f6787511" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499459 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of boys:\n\n$$\n121⟶120\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the number of boys to the number of girls is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $120$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $40$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 40}{5\\times 40}=\\frac{120}{200}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $200$ girls in Eastwood High School." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499369 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1069 "q-345097" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1071 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1072 #uuid "3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499460 1074 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{42}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{144}{25})}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499424 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1069 "q-82157" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1071 "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1072 #uuid "3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499461 1074 "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{a}{2x})}^{3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{a}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499380 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1069 "q-143485" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{148}$ be a random sample of size $n=148$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =98$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =74.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2843\\,$ | $\\,0.2810\\,$ | $\\,0.2776\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ | $\\,0.3156\\,$ | $\\,0.3121\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ | $\\,0.3483\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1072 #uuid "404778b6-2da1-56e5-aa20-574c3d5c8b29" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499462 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=148,\\,\\mu =98,\\,\\sigma =74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(98,\\frac{{74}^{2}}{148}) \\\\  & ∼N(98,37).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =P(Z>\\frac{95-98}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.49) & =1-P(Z<-0.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.49)=0.3121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =1-Φ(-0.49) \\\\  & =1-0.3121 \\\\  & =0.6879.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499463 1069 "e-51599" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499463 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?\n\n![](images/db/db06ddd333f1212337e37a5090aa50607f35c210768614b2c76becb267a438b5.svg) ![](images/70/70735c1de4ef1fd1c52dc25425367780dacb19dd268c9d21c774a15cb