![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/7c/7cd932842d9ae0d5fc425ee041b38155430ef8cca572adf3c9e4e4d2983a617e.svg) and ![](images/42/42e829426bf6a9f4c1a5afda3bf7d39f1cd3fa3c58b05aa37a2103e440081a32.svg)\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) So, it is either ![](images/c9/c983181e93167656339df404a2031696e0409b558cf70676ed574e4367cf5ed6.svg) or ![](images/d8/d86b01270851dbe9d7eae65f51266ecad5b7361429ee5d131acb5f0e9e58cd5e.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/b9/b9545d45e50201e2e348eea7a79a0e8a480765e53c75cc619306f71569413a32.svg) we know that ![](images/a0/a06ddf203ca30569ef240823ab98f890e6418e528eb5b4a1e87074b52c500d0b.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/ba/ba1ca097eda9cfed0062b1845069809f38d1a6a67e0b2adcea778090d5d4bb36.svg) is the greatest number among the given options." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499464 1069 "e-2284" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499464 1071 "The number of pears and apples in Peter's greengrocer is shown in the table below.\n\n| Apples | Pears |\n| --- | --- |\n| $42$ | $81$ |\n\nThere are approximately $60$ pears in Maria's greengrocer.\nThe ratio of the number of apples to the number of pears is equal in both greengrocers.\nBy rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of apples that are in Maria's greengrocer." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499464 1072 #uuid "43fbc756-3ac8-517b-a6c5-99ae38b706c1" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499464 1074 "First, let's round the number of pears and apples in Peter's greengrocer:\n\n- Apples: $42 \\longrightarrow 40$\n\n- Pears: $81 \\longrightarrow 80$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of apples to the number of pears (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{80} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the number of apples, and the denominator represents the number of pears.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{80 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe know that there are approximately $60$ pears in Maria's greengrocer, so we want to make a denominator of $60.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $30\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 \\times 30}{2 \\times 30} = \\dfrac{30}{60}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $30$ apples in Maria's greengrocer." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499376 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1069 "q-87761" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1071 "![](images/9a/9a4182f96ede84fdae27964aa21fc0537a37e279ec7eb7c390aeb94d1cae8eb9.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the pentagons shown in the diagram above?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1072 #uuid "486306f3-8fb2-5f6e-b7bd-26ff06793aaa" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499465 1074 "Notice that $∠W≅∠Z.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second pentagon that correspond to $Z.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YZ & =MN \\\\ ZV & =NJ \\\\ ∠Z & ≅∠N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $Z$ corresponds to vertex $N,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $W$ corresponds to vertex $K.$\n\nAs for the other three vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠X & ≅∠L \\\\ ∠Y & ≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =ML \\\\ WX & =LK.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $X$ corresponds to vertex $L,$\n\n- vertex $Y$ corresponds to vertex $M,$ and\n\n- vertex $V$ corresponds to vertex $J$ because they both correspond to the right angle.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nVWXYZ≅JKLMN.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499421 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499431 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1069 "q-556447" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1072 #uuid "4cff9fda-568d-5f3b-ab6b-f2c93ac02430" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499466 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $10$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies.\nWe know that ${2}^{3}=8$ and ${3}^{3}=27$.\nSince $8<10<27$, we know that $2<\\sqrt[3]{10}<3$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $2$ and $3$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.0}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2.1}^{3} & =9.261 \\\\ {2.2}^{3} & =10.648 \\\\ {2.3}^{3} & =12.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9.261<10<10.648$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{2.1}^{3}<10<{2.2}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n2.1<\\sqrt[3]{10}<2.2\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between $2.1$ and $2.2$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=2.1$ and $b=2.2$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499336 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1069 "q-345169" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1071 "![](images/d3/d34d844ed99307f03ff2454f8916efa91738d7b967f546ef6e516e003d4fc481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1072 #uuid "4f1e7e70-00e5-5263-ad86-e1c271d552fe" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499467 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499345 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1069 "q-555452" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{40}$ lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1072 #uuid "4f73f19a-494d-5194-ba7a-fdcda5923013" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499468 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $40.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $40$ are $36$ and $49.$ We have\n\n$$\n36<40<49.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & <\\sqrt{40}<\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 6 & <\\sqrt{40}<7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5c/5c2775762ff274451dfd3258bacbede7240e6bad25b782cc86a76484e61d31bb.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499346 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1069 "q-237269" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1071 "![](images/38/3824c1dbfa375eebd47d2bfe5f0367e0b71d5fe570f06c67c7b8bfac884cb33d.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠DGB?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1072 #uuid "51d13ed4-ff4e-5ab5-b96d-412aa0f7d847" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499469 1074 "![](images/60/608776ee1428cae51b4831120bec072890c118799a365a3fb53aeff12deec07e.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠BGA$ is supplementary to $∠DGB$ in the given diagram." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499384 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1069 "q-345162" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q=11.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1072 #uuid "5218de49-0b02-5048-960b-3418b12d24b2" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499470 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499394 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1069 "q-53484" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1071 "![](images/d4/d46182f3d4373a6b8bb1d330afb51a4ed2eea0c33b2f929b5538d7e0edf76353.png)\n\nGiven that $ABCDE≅MPQRT,$ find $x-2y.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1072 #uuid "56adf5b0-549f-5613-9c35-0245d2557d7d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499471 1074 "The statement $ABCDE≅MPQRT$ means that $P$ and $Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $M,$\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $P,$\n\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Q,$\n\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $R,$\n\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MP & =AB \\\\ y-4 & =2 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠E & =m∠T \\\\ {(3x+20)}^{∘} & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+20 & =140 \\\\ 3x & =120 \\\\ x & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx-2y=40-12=28.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499314 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1069 "q-59862" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1071 "Sam and Justin get paid for mowing neighborhood lawns. The ratio of Sam's reward to Justin's reward is $2:3.$ \nDuring a particular week, Sam received $$416.$ By rounding Sam's earnings to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of money that Justin received." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1072 #uuid "57ef88a7-22a6-5c86-bd20-7f67801a22ad" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499472 1074 "First, let's approximate the amount of money that Sam received:\n\n$$\n$416⟶$400\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Sam's earnings to Justin's earnings is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Sam's earnings  and the denominator represents Justin's earnings.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $400$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 200}{3\\times 200}=\\frac{400}{600}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Justin received approximately $$600.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499392 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1069 "q-116487" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1071 "The fuel cost $C,$ in dollars per hour, for running a train is directly proportional to the **square** of its speed $v,$ measured in miles per hour. If it costs $$15$ per hour to run a train that moves at a speed of $100$ miles per hour, what equation models this situation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1072 #uuid "58ce1cea-1782-532b-bc46-409e39fb6f1c" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499473 1074 "The fuel cost $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the speed $v,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=15$ dollars per hour when $v=100\\,\\text{mph}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\cdot {100}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 10\\,000 \\\\ \\frac{15}{10\\,000} & =k \\\\ 0.0015 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=0.0015{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499371 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1069 "q-237296" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1071 "![](images/34/3400186b82e707bd580fc57a6c90912b37509580f971d7b0f288b3576cef5cf7.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠COB?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1072 #uuid "58d8817e-1ed5-560b-ab5e-ef6a013cd811" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499474 1074 "![](images/3b/3b1fc84f667fa48ee10952f96f3d17bd23ba2f5c29f529053378d59741e573ad.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠BOE$ is supplementary to $∠COB$ in the given diagram." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499311 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1069 "q-120128" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1071 "![](images/a0/a09bde30c164f4ca5bf964690e734057efe0344f1136c611d04492a85cc8d4bb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1072 #uuid "5a54090b-41ad-5e7f-a026-590e30e34f07" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499475 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a triangle (it has $3$ sides). So, the polygons $R$ and $S$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $3$ sides.\n\n- The polygons $U$ and  $Q$ are *not* congruent to $P$ (even though they both have $3$ sides) because $U$ and $Q$ don't have the same shape with $P.$\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $T$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅T.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499379 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1069 "q-143476" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{90}$ be a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =92$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =30.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $90$ and $95.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1072 #uuid "5ae46753-9928-51e1-ad50-7c5a5efcdfe5" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499476 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499317 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1069 "q-60031" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1071 "The following table shows the number of families in a town that have a dog and the number of families that do not.\n\n| Families who have a dog | Families that do not have a dog |\n| --- | --- |\n| $151$ | $92$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of the number of families that have a dog to those that do not." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1072 #uuid "5b830f36-6958-507b-9f99-7670b58c2e50" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499477 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of families who have a dog and those who do not by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- Families who have a dog: $151⟶150$\n\n- Families who do not have a dog: $92⟶90$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog to those who do not is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{90}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150÷10}{90÷10}=\\frac{15}{9}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15÷3}{9÷3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nHence, the ratio of the number of families who have a dog and who do not have is approximately $5:3.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499349 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1069 "q-555465" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1072 #uuid "5ccb9537-59db-525f-88c4-f6dfbe10d934" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499478 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{85},$ we first locate the perfect cubes that are closest to $85.$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3} & =64 \\\\ {5}^{3} & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85$ is between $64$ and $125,$ its cube root must be between $4$ and $5.$ This eliminates any choices that do not fall in this range.\n\n![](images/9b/9b97872f5a4258a5b417f6e91bfc33733125b7b0f16299b6cddf06ce3f6cd87b.png)\n\nWe can further refine our estimate by considering distances. The number $85$ is closer to $64$ than it is to $125,$ so we expect its cube root to be closer to $4$ than to $5.$ \n\nLooking at the remaining choices—$4.1,$ $4.4,$ and $4.7$—we can test a value by cubing it to see how close the result is to $85.$ Let's test $4.4$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4.4}^{3} & =4.4\\cdot 4.4\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =19.36\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =85.184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85.184$ is very close to $85,$ we can confidently choose $4.4$ as our estimate.\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}$ is $4.4.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499310 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1069 "q-82910" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1071 "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1072 #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499479 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{3} \\\\ v={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499322 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1069 "q-60072" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1071 "The following table shows the number of babies born at a hospital in New York during a particular month:\n\n| Boys | Girls |\n| --- | --- |\n| $102$ | $81$ |\n\nApproximately $120$ girls were born at a hospital in Boston during the same month. The ratio of boys to girls was the same at both hospitals. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of boys born at the Boston hospital during that month." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1072 #uuid "5ec248fa-4181-5322-854b-62b56bba8e0b" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499480 1074 "First, let's round the number of babies born in the New York Hospital to the nearest $10$:\n\n- Boys: $102⟶100$\n\n- Girls: $81⟶80$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of boys to the number of girls (expressed as a fraction) is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{100}{80}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{100÷10}{80÷10}=\\frac{10}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10÷2}{8÷2}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $120$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $30$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 30}{4\\times 30}=\\frac{150}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $150$ boys were born at Boston Hospital in that particular month." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499403 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1069 "q-556439" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1072 #uuid "5ec75543-e826-5114-b0c0-b95399a3601d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499481 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{73},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $73.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<73<81,$ we know that the square root of $73$ lies between $8$ and $9.$ This tells us that $7.6$ and $36.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $73$ is slightly closer to $81$ than to $64$ (the distance to $64$ is $9,$ and the distance to $81$ is $8$), we expect $\\sqrt{73}$ to be near the middle of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $73:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.2}^{2} & =67.24 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25 \\\\ {8.9}^{2} & =79.21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${8.5}^{2}=72.25$ is very close to $73.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}$ is $8.5.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499387 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1069 "q-345164" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q=25.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1072 #uuid "62aefe8f-6088-586d-94c3-d6e202d70a8e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499482 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4}=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499326 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1069 "q-142145" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{y}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1072 #uuid "64c8a090-cafc-5e88-b591-72ecb0cc7b81" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499483 1074 "First, we rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{y}={y}^{1/2}\\cdot {y}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot {y}^{1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{1/2+1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3/4} & =\\sqrt[4]{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499360 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1069 "q-9534" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1071 "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1072 #uuid "657b78ff-bfd8-5861-b182-c1815c474783" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499484 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{6\\theta }+36{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{40{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499420 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1069 "q-345156" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1072 #uuid "676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499485 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{21}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ 2=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499395 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1069 "q-349587" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1071 "The following table shows the width and length, in meters, of a rectangular storage warehouse.\n\n| Width | Length |\n| --- | --- |\n| $843$ | $1\\,617$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of width to length for this warehouse." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1072 #uuid "67be50ef-178a-58b6-89ed-7bbd8ca38af8" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499486 1074 "First, let's approximate the width and length by rounding to the nearest $100$:\n\n- width: $843⟶800$\n\n- length: $1\\,617⟶1\\,600$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of width to length (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{800}{1\\,600}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{800÷100}{1\\,600÷100}=\\frac{8}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $8$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8÷8}{16÷8}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of width to length is approximately $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499357 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1069 "q-87683" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1071 "![](images/23/2375302a1591f251e33b1d3813e8ea500ce0f7cb85082513d67c30e09e5b8670.png)\n\nGiven the polygons shown above, which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $C≅V$\n\nII. $V≅B$\n\nIII. $B≅C$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1072 #uuid "6b6253c5-dbcb-5eca-bada-e65b2d371ca0" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499487 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the polygons $C$ and $V$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent sides.\n\n- Statement II is false. By the same reasoning as statement I, we can see that the polygons $V$ and $B$ are not congruent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the polygons $B$ and $C$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $B≅C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499329 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499393 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499411 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1069 "q-556452" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{20},$ $\\sqrt{5},$ and $\\sqrt{11}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1072 #uuid "6c62dfe4-3d3e-58f1-bc84-569e3d239868" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499488 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{5}:$ \nSince $4<5<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{5}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{11}:$\nSince $9<11<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{11}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{20}:$\nSince $16<20<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{20}<\\sqrt{25}.$ This means $4<\\sqrt{20}<5.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n2<\\sqrt{5}<3<\\sqrt{11}<4<\\sqrt{20}<5\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{5},$ $\\sqrt{11},$ and $\\sqrt{20}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499353 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1069 "q-556440" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1072 #uuid "6f1f35ac-b2a3-5225-badd-5521e9e9745c" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499489 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{29},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $29.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<29<36,$ we know that the square root of $29$ lies between $5$ and $6.$ This tells us that $4.9$ and $14.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $29$ is much closer to $25$ than to $36$ (the distance to $25$ is $4,$ and the distance to $36$ is $7$), we expect $\\sqrt{29}$ to be near the lower end of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.7}^{2} & =32.49 \\\\ {5.9}^{2} & =34.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${5.4}^{2}=29.16$ is very close to $29.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}$ is $5.4.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499433 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1069 "q-160638" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1071 "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1072 #uuid "77ed6de7-60cd-57e5-95ef-8b1b61a0f9cb" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499490 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/61/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499324 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1069 "q-161893" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n∭(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1072 #uuid "787730fc-5924-57cd-ac3a-f6e87e085583" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499491 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499356 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1069 "q-142971" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1071 "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =43.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-280,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1072 #uuid "795def41-6dcd-54b5-9baf-91c8a014878e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499492 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -280\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{43}{\\sqrt{45}} \\\\  & -280\\pm 12.564\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499351 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1069 "q-87636" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1071 "![](images/81/81c7a4f9f0b9ab855b22ad7895f5f0d7b1ae3711563e9691117dfd99f878dccb.png)\n\nGiven the polygon $P$ shown above, which of the following polygons is congruent to $P?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1072 #uuid "7ca08efa-e8ff-5fd7-8af8-59ae906150bd" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499493 1074 "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare each of the given polygons to $P.$\n\n- The polygon $P$ is a pentagon (it has $5$ sides). So, the polygons $S,$ $T,$ and $U$ can't be congruent to the polygon $P$ because they do not have $5$ sides.\n\n- The polygon $Q$ is *not* congruent to $\\,P$ (even though they both have $5$ sides) because $Q$ and $P$ don't have the same shape. In particular, notice that $P$ has two right angles, while $Q$ doesn't have right angles.\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ and $R$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $P≅R.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499378 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1069 "q-345133" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1071 "![](images/12/12a73555c78b324406714337b999b12a2166559eb1795236854e29747e88a5b1.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1072 #uuid "7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499494 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-6\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},$ $\\beta =\\frac{2}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+2=-1.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499495 1069 "e-51600" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499495 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499495 1072 #uuid "809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-