cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/c4/c4a4623be7f3d5660215b04af294514485d84bef208d5097f723feb72ec328da.svg) and ![](images/83/83ba2899aad41f580bf181cc53d40d2a1fa4fb881acd86fb09324b9dd4027550.svg) so ![](images/de/de19f2fd6bae35a5893d4ac5e7704c12750044637eb4750842141f5b1d0b15a9.svg)\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to ![](images/95/9591360f4ce469dc90934057a5ea17d0eaaa61ba52a1bb515a21d0a41fcdb966.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/aa/aad398006b3c5b9cf716a7455bc6aa9f7abc68b57a5de763efb7c7f5928a9547.svg) is ![](images/61/61d92f48929a5d8d900087d1765a4b82640a5f93fbe9124879e0ba5fa4954a24.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/92/92ab44e79c1791a780944b6449d9a8af8c43fbd4e7323bf7f995dca1fc5f45da.svg) is ![](images/51/51197b9d01e2d0c848a9f8728f00a6dc518521d749848d5a4d2816f1d8e06aa3.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) is much closer to ![](images/3a/3a199a75daabe803a6adb7279c9b2b4022a0c2989e41b5611b478e2d8322c4f9.svg) ![](images/36/36c2915c61e115b1d5c8f055defd37ee7e73858145e203088a49d5a0d79a6e23.svg) is closer to ![](images/bf/bff510d56c2a8598a777cec76166de972132e5d73f2ef73dcb2983b00bdac1e9.svg)\n\nTherefore, rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place, ![](images/b7/b7f0378eb41d1ca9f785366f04d5b1b11a9632cd40d694140c235f0033ab0bcb.svg)" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499309 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1069 "q-345106" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1072 #uuid "80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499497 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58cde9ddaa098a14ebb26bb70128f018f0c5892214815cc2d3057c9881a9546f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499498 1069 "e-4892" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499498 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499498 1072 #uuid "83d31d9c-626c-5711-b7cf-1417f78d3347" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499498 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lies between, we can compare ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is ![](images/67/67b1b7ef88f956cc914bdc85cacbd83df6a22c511224e85d3d9aeeb80eee9392.svg) since ![](images/e9/e9c62ca9ae68e481505f45bff035d951215eb0beea7f3289b7b9f550bdc99560.svg)\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is ![](images/3b/3b0ce4686cd13ee54ad467f989a69a337a7037b78fd2a14d562a1d299ba1e4b5.svg) since ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\nThis means ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is between ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) and ![](images/54/54f588e00853e81c9aececc72d9474b6816aaec1c979ca3d940c0030c2e401ab.svg)\n![](images/eb/eb5e875b9bf93096c7a1745a02fae75a8be75ab827b1f624fab5e2804cdedb73.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n![](images/9d/9d2b62267e10537690522252c069b2a24b1e95b92321ddad314ada26aa0114b6.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499402 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1069 "q-92858" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1071 "The following table shows the weight, in kilograms, of sand and crushed stone in a concrete mix.\n\n| Sand | Crushed stone |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,224$ | $1\\,645$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the weight of sand to the weight of the crushed stone." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1072 #uuid "842009cb-80b4-53ea-a070-3fcd9cc4ab48" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499499 1074 "First, let's approximate the weight of sand and crushed stone by rounding to the nearest $100$:\n\n- sand: $1\\,224⟶1\\,200$\n\n- crushed stone: $1\\,645⟶1\\,600$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the weight of sand to the weight of the crushed stone is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,200}{1\\,600}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,200÷100}{1\\,600÷100}=\\frac{12}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷4}{16÷4}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the weight of sand to the weight of the crushed stone is approximately $\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499426 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1069 "q-72511" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1071 "Simplify the expression $y\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{y}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1072 #uuid "842d5936-85a3-5b6e-b4fa-f505a9d0860b" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499500 1074 "First, we rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{y}=y\\cdot {y}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot {y}^{1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{1+1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{5/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5/4} & =\\sqrt[4]{{y}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499410 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1069 "q-364191" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1071 "Rachel and Tomas work at a bakery. The ratio of Rachel's sales to Tomas's sales is $7:9.$ \nDuring a particular week, Tomas completed $912$ sales. By rounding Tomas's sales to the nearest hundred, approximate the number of sales that Rachel completed." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1072 #uuid "84690951-1e12-55e6-b7b2-8d0eed282ba4" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499501 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of sales that Tomas completed:\n\n$$\n912⟶900\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Rachel's sales to Tomas's sales is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Rachel's sales  and the denominator represents Tomas's sales.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $900$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 100}{9\\times 100}=\\frac{700}{900}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Rachel completed approximately $700$ sales." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499425 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1069 "q-15123" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1071 "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1072 #uuid "85d77d15-6969-5117-952d-e2b202d58514" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499502 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499366 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1069 "q-82144" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1071 "The expression $\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}}$ is equivalent to" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1072 #uuid "860064d1-6642-51e5-83a1-e8a0a0b4fe77" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499503 1074 "The expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}} & =8-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499342 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1069 "q-162537" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1072 #uuid "8662a311-148d-52aa-9581-67da3a804b43" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499504 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499429 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1069 "q-3928" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1071 "${(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3}=$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1072 #uuid "86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499505 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3}{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({u}^{6})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {3}^{3\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {u}^{6\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {v}^{(-3/5)\\cdot (-2/3)} & = \\\\ {3}^{-6/3}\\cdot {u}^{-12/3}\\cdot {v}^{6/15} & = \\\\ {3}^{-2}\\cdot {u}^{-4}\\cdot {v}^{2/5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{{3}^{2}{u}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{9{u}^{4}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499335 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499383 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1069 "q-556462" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1072 #uuid "8b525db8-241b-5276-b6b9-4b8689047d2b" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499506 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $110.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $110$ are $100$ and $121.$ We have\n\n$$\n100<110<121.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{100} & <\\sqrt{110}<\\sqrt{121} \\\\ 10 & <\\sqrt{110}<11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9bef06cb0000173c30147f4f9f633f3adec207b688adb005bcf0fff1aa8d7b13.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $10$ and $11.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=10$ and $b=11.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499330 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1069 "q-556486" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{20}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1072 #uuid "8b9de470-595d-5301-8412-daba962c6a6e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499507 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ we first find the two perfect cubes closest to $20.$\n\nThe closest perfect cubes are $8={2}^{3}$ and $27={3}^{3}.$\n\nSince $8<20<27,$ we know that the cube root of $20$ lies between $2$ and $3.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4eb7ee1951d0d402df1553d46bc6e8ce1fdb421adc40949b9e79964484892c6.png)\n\nBecause $20$ is closer to $27$ than to $8,$ we expect $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ to be close to $3.$ \n\nLet's test a decimal near $3,$ such as $2.7,$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n{2.7}^{3}=19.683\n$$\n\nSince $19.683$ is slightly less than $20,$ we know that $2.7<\\sqrt[3]{20}<2.8.$\n\nNext, we determine whether $20$ is closer to ${2.7}^{3}$ or ${2.8}^{3}:$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.7}^{3}$ is $20-19.683=0.317.$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.8}^{3}$ is $21.952-20=1.952.$\n\nSince $20$ is much closer to $19.683,$ $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ is closer to $2.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt[3]{20}≈2.7.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499327 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1069 "q-141984" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1071 "${({a}^{2}b)}^{3}\\cdot ab=$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1072 #uuid "8bcb7c88-fa9c-5de4-82d6-48872f12ed89" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499508 1074 "We start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({a}^{2}b)}^{3}\\cdot ab=({a}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}\\cdot ab & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}\\cdot ab & = \\\\ {a}^{2\\cdot 3}\\cdot {b}^{3}\\cdot ab & = \\\\ {a}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{3}\\cdot ab & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{3}\\cdot ab & = \\\\ ({a}^{6}\\cdot a)\\cdot ({b}^{3}\\cdot b) & = \\\\ {a}^{6+1}\\cdot {b}^{3+1} & = \\\\ {a}^{7}\\cdot {b}^{4} & = \\\\ {a}^{7}{b}^{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499398 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1069 "q-345128" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1071 "![](images/fd/fd23aac9a3d8025ff12c84b2e2706814ed998879a8e374222a08181d6d88b5bf.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1072 #uuid "8d50a041-6cbe-5974-9378-7af01eb141c4" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499509 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $18\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.125\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n5(0)+18(0)+8q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499432 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1069 "q-162621" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1072 #uuid "8e84ccf0-2025-55a1-91ce-ccafeeadc53e" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499510 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=x-1$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=0$ or $g(y)=-y,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=xy-y$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-y & =c \\\\ y(x-1) & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=1$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dcf779041bbae1ce0a6c89042277b4dc33bb1eabe16d42a7824945a118951e0b.png)" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499401 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1069 "q-555461" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1072 #uuid "92983ecf-db5c-5c74-b988-756fcd6337e6" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499511 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $45$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<45<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{45}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.4}^{3} & =39.304 \\\\ {3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $42.875<45<46.656$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.5}^{3}<45<{3.6}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.5<\\sqrt[3]{45}<3.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies between $3.5$ and $3.6$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499375 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1069 "q-162210" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1072 #uuid "92990ecc-5ba0-56e3-944d-6e84f299eb57" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499512 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-{e}^{x},\\,v=y,\\,w=z{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & 0 & {e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & {e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499390 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1069 "q-364167" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1071 "Emma and Noah get paid for mowing lawns. The ratio of Emma's reward to Noah's reward is $7:3.$ \nDuring a particular week, Noah received $$1215.$ By rounding Noah's earnings to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of money that Emma received." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1072 #uuid "989f692a-e9cc-5d93-9c14-352947770c60" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499513 1074 "First, let's approximate the amount of money that Noah received:\n\n$$\n$1215⟶$1200\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Emma's earnings to Noah's earnings is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Emma's earnings  and the denominator represents Noah's earnings.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1200$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $400$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 400}{3\\times 400}=\\frac{2800}{1200}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Emma received approximately $$2800.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499399 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1069 "q-72553" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(7{x}^{-5})}^{2}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1072 #uuid "997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499514 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7{x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {7}^{2}{({x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ 49\\cdot {x}^{-5\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ 49{x}^{-10} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{x}^{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499361 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1069 "q-345126" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{16}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1072 #uuid "9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499515 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/23/23e8796d3977632278d4d393510a30df974634750a1c02f56fd8bacbe3b5d57a.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I=32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/16)}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q=12.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n8(0)+32(0)+16q=12,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n16q=12\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499374 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1069 "q-345111" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1071 "![](images/8f/8f6c545a697bfa58794995f802950e509e3df63bd3d34db957ee3556461ec03f.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1072 #uuid "9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499516 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499307 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1069 "q-816" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1071 "${(2{x}^{-2}y)}^{-3}=$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1072 #uuid "9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499517 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{-2}y)^{-3} & = \\\\ {2}^{-3}{({x}^{-2})}^{-3}{({y}^{1})}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}\\cdot {x}^{(-2)(-3)}\\cdot {y}^{(1)(-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {x}^{6}\\cdot {y}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499419 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1069 "q-164396" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1071 "Identify the surface $4{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+{z}^{2}=1.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1072 #uuid "a43bb9df-6cd4-59cd-9e78-40afc4cecca9" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499518 1074 "Recall that the equation of an ellipsoid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an ellipsoid with $a=\\frac{1}{2},\\,b=3,\\,c=1.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499323 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1069 "q-4296" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1071 "![](images/03/03dcc36a9c600b3c6fcb7eb08e6cc8f79bde0d72fa1942e370646b1ae4ef0e9e.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $∠DGF?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1072 #uuid "a6e88d08-4c58-5e8b-a893-b780f6e53988" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499519 1074 "![](images/f5/f5760b72b51933c5ab7acba8441c2f86185dddfbd8ac60b46e5c36b985b38b2e.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $∠BGD$ is supplementary to $∠DGF$ in the given diagram." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499520 1069 "e-2281" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499520 1071 "The following table shows the number of children in a park who like baseball.\n\n| Children who like baseball | Children who don't like baseball |\n| --- | --- |\n| $43$ | $69$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499520 1072 #uuid "a8368c62-f936-54c5-b069-1adabcb11ffe" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499520 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of children who like baseball and those who don't like it by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Children who like baseball: $43 \\longrightarrow 40$\n\n- Children who don't like baseball: $69 \\longrightarrow 70$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{70} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{70 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately $\\dfrac{4}{7},$ or $4:7.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499423 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1069 "q-164513" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1071 "Identify the surface $y={x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1072 #uuid "aac5719a-7a14-5fc9-ab07-f4320aad4083" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499521 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis and $a=1,\\,c=2.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499354 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1069 "q-236468" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1071 "The force $F,$ measured in newtons $(\\text{N})$, needed to extend a spring by some length $l,$ measured in $\\text{m},$ is directly proportional to $l.$ When the extension of the spring is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force on the spring is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ What is the constant of variation?\n\nThe constant of variation is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{N/m}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1072 #uuid "ab23edc2-70be-5457-b5af-37bf9a82da09" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499522 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the extension $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF=kl,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the extension is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =k\\cdot (0.005) \\\\ 25 & =0.005k \\\\ \\frac{25}{0.005} & =k \\\\ 5\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{F}{l},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}} & =\\text{N/m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=5\\,000\\,\\text{N/m}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499358 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1069 "q-162185" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1072 #uuid "ad40770e-3352-5877-ade7-ccfd965beccc" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499523 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-y,\\,v=x,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499415 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1069 "q-104671" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{y}^{9}}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{7}}}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1072 #uuid "af52435b-8d7b-5fb6-b052-723de06fafd6" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499524 1074 "First, we rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{9}}{\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{7}}}=\\frac{{y}^{9}}{{({y}^{7})}^{1/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{9}}{{({y}^{7})}^{1/3}}=\\frac{{y}^{9}}{{y}^{7(1/3)}}=\\frac{{y}^{9}}{{y}^{7/3}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{9}}{{y}^{7/3}} & = \\\\ {y}^{9-7/3} & = \\\\ {y}^{20/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{20/3} & =\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499416 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1069 "q-93275" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1071 "The building model has the same ratio of length to width as a real building\nThe following table shows the length and width of the building model in centimeters. \n\n| Length | Width |\n| --- | --- |\n| $84$ | $41$ |\n\nBy rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the length of the building if it is known that its width is $20$ meters." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1072 #uuid "b402cb00-d472-5e9f-9484-7067afb16406" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499525 1074 "First, let's round the length and width of the building model:\n\n- Length: $84⟶80$\n\n- Width: $41⟶40$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the length to the width (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{40}.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the length of the building model, and the denominator represents the width.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80÷10}{40÷10}=\\frac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 20}{1\\times 20}=\\frac{40}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the building is approximately $40$ meters." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499407 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1069 "q-60005" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1071 "The following table shows the number of boys and girls at Lincoln High School.\n\n| Boys | Girls |\n| --- | --- |\n| $331$ | $457$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the number of girls to the number of boys." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1072 #uuid "b48042cf-85c0-5d86-9405-062b1a5605f0" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499526 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of boys and girls by rounding to the nearest hundred.\n\n- Boys: $331⟶300$\n\n- Girls: $457⟶500$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls to the number of boys is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{500}{300}.\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{500÷100}{300÷100}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls to the number of boys is approximately $\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499427 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1069 "q-72439" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1071 "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-xy=x+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1072 #uuid "b5a12ff0-24e0-5963-9f16-1e9a01270fba" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499527 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-xy & =x+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-xy-x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-xy-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-xy-x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-xy-x)j \\\\  & =(2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2y$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-(2y)y-(2y) & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ -{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=-1$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-1).$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499355 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1069 "q-555464" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1071 "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1072 #uuid "b73b2161-1f16-50bc-aeef-165cf5e2f96c" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499528 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{55},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $55.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $49={7}^{2}$ and $64={8}^{2}.$\n\nSince $49<55<64,$ we know that the square root of $55$ lies between $7$ and $8.$ This tells us that $6.8$ and $27.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $55$ is slightly closer to $49$ than to $64$ (the distance to $49$ is $6,$ and the distance to $64$ is $9$), we expect $\\sqrt{55}$ to be near the middle, but slightly less than $7.5.$ \n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $55:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7.1}^{2} & =50.41 \\\\ {7.4}^{2} & =54.76 \\\\ {7.9}^{2} & =62.41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${7.4}^{2}=54.76$ is very close to $55.$ \n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}$ is $7.4.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499428 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1069 "q-65205" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1071 "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{y}-1=xy.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1072 #uuid "b96af04a-99a9-59d6-b4b4-fac54542ff08" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499529 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}-1 & =xy \\\\ x{e}^{y}-xy & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-xy)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-xy)j \\\\  & =(x{e}^{y}-x)i-({e}^{y}-y)j \\\\ t(1,0) & =((1){e}^{0}-(1))i-({e}^{0}-(0))j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-j.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499400 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1069 "q-59865" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1071 "The table below shows the numbers of students in a school who wear glasses and those who do not.\n\n| Wear Glasses | Don't Wear Glasses |\n| --- | --- |\n| $117$ | $361$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nBy rounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1072 #uuid "b9cc6d27-c2ce-5a44-925e-4c2f0fca32a7" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499530 1074 "First, let's approximate the total number of students who wear glasses and those who don't by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- wear glasses: $117⟶120$\n\n- don't wear Glasses: $361⟶360$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{120}{360}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{120÷10}{360÷10}=\\frac{12}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $12:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷12}{36÷12}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of students who wear glasses to those who don’t is approximately $1:3.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499320 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1069 "q-59992" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1071 "The following table shows the weight, in grams, of wheat flour and sugar in a cake mix.\n\n| Wheat Flour | Sugar |\n| --- | --- |\n| $615$ | $408$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest hundred, determine the approximate ratio of the weight of wheat flour to the weight of the mix of wheat flour and sugar." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1072 #uuid "ba8fb786-4538-58b4-8d18-8c109a83d218" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499531 1074 "First, let's approximate the weight of wheat flour and sugar by rounding to the nearest $100$:\n\n- Wheat flour: $615⟶600$\n\n- Sugar: $408⟶400$\n\nThus, the weight of the mix of these two ingredients is approximately $600+400=1\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the weight of the wheat flour to the weight of the mix is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{600}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{600÷100}{1\\,000÷100}=\\frac{6}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the weight of the wheat flour to the weight of the mix of wheat flour and sugar is approximately $\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499328 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1069 "q-116482" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1071 "At a car rental agency, the charge $C,$ in dollars, for renting a car varies directly with the **square** of the distance  $d$ traveled by the car, measured in miles. If it costs $$5$ to rent a car to drive $20$ miles, then what equation models this situation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1072 #uuid "bcc34549-4b02-5866-8337-252ca34764e3" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499532 1074 "The charge $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{d}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=$5$ when $d=20\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =k\\cdot {20}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =k\\cdot 400 \\\\ \\frac{5}{400} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{80} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{80}{d}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499377 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1069 "q-87760" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1071 "![](images/7c/7c1663caeea9751ccbaa2544792c9c15331accedc6dd757b44154673f34f8ddc.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1072 #uuid "c4dcd0bc-d6f0-59ef-aa66-febcad580925" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499533 1074 "Notice that $∠M≅∠O.$ \n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $M.$ First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =DE \\\\ MP & =DG \\\\ ∠M & ≅∠D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- vertex $M$ corresponds to vertex $D,$\n\n- consequently, vertex $O$ corresponds to vertex $F.$\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠N & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠P & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OP & =FG \\\\ ON & =FE,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $N$ corresponds to $E,$ and\n\n- vertex $P$ corresponds to $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nMNOP≅DEFG.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499386 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1069 "q-143482" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =250$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =64.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $260.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ | $\\,0.9699\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1072 #uuid "c5ea7895-e704-5589-b13a-69dbf76447fe" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499534 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499318 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1069 "q-162615" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=({y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y)\\,i+({x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+4{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i-3y\\,j$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1072 #uuid "c7c0eb25-897e-5093-b34f-0455f3db55f9" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499535 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-6xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-6xy)+6xy=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =16{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+16{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-3)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499412 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1069 "q-555455" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1071 "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1072 #uuid "c82eb124-d241-562e-a9aa-60dccb777294" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499536 1074 "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt{29}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{40}$ lies between $3$ and $4,$ since ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{90}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{20}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{2}=16$ and ${5}^{2}=25.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $5$ and $6.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt{29}$ or $\\sqrt{27}.$ \n\nSince $29>27,$ we know that $\\sqrt{29}>\\sqrt{27}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{29}$ is the greatest number among the given options." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499337 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1069 "q-143509" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0012\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1072 #uuid "cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499537 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499308 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1069 "q-143484" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{98}$ be a random sample of size $n=98$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =180$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =70.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $182.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5279\\,$ | $\\,0.5319\\,$ | $\\,0.5359\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ | $\\,0.5714\\,$ | $\\,0.5753\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ | $\\,0.6103\\,$ | $\\,0.6141\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1072 #uuid "cb19bbbb-4b9e-595b-b617-d56f35ac7f98" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499538 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=98,\\,\\mu =180,\\,\\sigma =70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(180,\\frac{{70}^{2}}{98}) \\\\  & ∼N(180,50).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>182).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =P(Z>\\frac{182-180}{\\sqrt{50}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.28) & =1-P(Z<0.28) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.28).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28)=0.6103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =1-Φ(0.28) \\\\  & =1-0.6103 \\\\  & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499344 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1069 "q-345145" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1071 "![](images/0e/0e03b65f8301526be5ff2ce5ec3c7f8b896906bfef2a6308d2125e93f0ecf631.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1072 #uuid "cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499539 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{36}{145})}^{2}+{(\\frac{32}{145})}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499312 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499333 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1069 "q-556458" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1071 "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1072 #uuid "cefb6c0d-04aa-5eeb-98b9-43235f7ba892" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499540 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect cubes that are just below and just above $30.$\n\nThe perfect cubes closest to $30$ are $27$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n27<30<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27} & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 3 & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/3e/3efc92b2664e8fade27edc67f977d78b317e73343d3201ac38c1fad807cdb01a.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $3$ and $4.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=3$ and $b=4.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499343 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1069 "q-60069" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1071 "The number of dogs and cats in shelter A is shown in the following table:\n\n| Dogs | Cats |\n| --- | --- |\n| $51$ | $32$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn shelter B there are approximately $20$ dogs. The ratio of the number of dogs to the number of cats in both shelters is the same. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of cats in shelter B." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1072 #uuid "d2ff2ee0-f6fe-5976-b859-e0adc12b8590" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499541 1074 "First, let's round the number of dogs and cats in shelter A:\n\n- Dogs: $51⟶50$\n\n- Cats: $32⟶30$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of dogs to the number of cats (expressed as a fraction) is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{50}{30}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of dogs and the denominator represents the number of cats.\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$ gives an equivalent fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{50÷10}{30÷10}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 4}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{20}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $12$ cats in shelter B." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499347 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1069 "q-345277" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1071 "![](images/3c/3c08141f91eea5b2d2006926de5239f91630de139fae970147d8267d46429392.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1072 #uuid "d36cf0d0-fde1-5898-8003-f96433332315" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499542 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta =30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499430 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1069 "q-120267" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1071 "The price $P,$ in dollars, of electricity in a certain city is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ measured in kilowatt-hour ($\\text{kWh}).$ When the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ What is the constant of variation?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1072 #uuid "d4a7e4f5-44ad-5538-a987-14a969b63b6d" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499543 1074 "The price $P$ is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=kC,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}264 & =k\\cdot (24) \\\\ 264 & =24k \\\\ \\frac{264}{24} & =k \\\\ 11 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{P}{C},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{kWh}} & =\\text{dollars per kilowatt-hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=$11$ per kilowatt-hour." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499341 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499396 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499405 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1069 "q-555914" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1072 #uuid "d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499544 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3}:$ \nSince $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4}.$ This means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{15}:$\nSince $9<15<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{15}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n1<\\sqrt{3}<2<\\sqrt{7}<3<\\sqrt{15}<4\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499315 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1069 "q-60058" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1071 "Marcus has two water tanks. Tank A has a capacity of $1\\,508$ liters. The ratio of the capacity of tank A to the capacity of tank B is $3:4.$ By rounding the capacity of tank A to the nearest hundred, approximate the capacity of tank B." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1072 #uuid "d5dd58d1-f976-5297-ad4f-4ae9b9a92e73" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499545 1074 "First, let's approximate the capacity of tank A:\n\n$$\n1\\,508⟶1\\,500\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the capacity of tank A to the capacity of tank B is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents the capacity of tank A and the denominator represents the capacity of tank B.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $1,500$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $500$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 500}{4\\times 500}=\\frac{1\\,500}{2\\,000}\n$$\n\nTherefore, tank B has a capacity of approximately $2\\,000$ liters." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499408 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1069 "q-51202" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1071 "![](images/87/87a3563153fe0310767a79a96df0df5f6c0824027776b761df4a792b8b9b235e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1072 #uuid "d774fdc1-484c-5e5e-b140-afbc35a348d0" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499546 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499364 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1069 "q-116379" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1071 "The amount of bleach $b,$ measured in gallons ($gal$), in a certain detergent product varies directly with the amount of water $w,$ measured in gallons, in the product. When the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ Find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is $w=9\\,gal.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1072 #uuid "db05b6d0-7f2f-569f-a84a-34026d2fb3c2" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499547 1074 "The amount of bleach $b$ is directly proportional to the amount of water $w,$ so we have\n\n$$\nb=kw.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.25 & =k\\cdot (3.75) \\\\ 1.25 & =3.75k \\\\ \\frac{1.25}{3.75} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}w.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is  $w=9\\,gal,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of bleach when $w=9\\,gal$ is $b=3\\,\\text{gal}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499409 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1069 "q-142987" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=35$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =9.4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=12.7,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1072 #uuid "dd631a49-72c7-508a-98fa-29863f38f5bf" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499548 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12.7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{9.4}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\  & 12.7\\pm 4.093\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499418 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1069 "q-22838" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}})}^{5}?$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1072 #uuid "e0110074-4c62-517f-b5cb-ff4cff4e0579" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499549 1074 "We start by using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}})}^{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule in the numerator and the power of product rule in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{2\\cdot 5}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {v}^{4\\cdot 5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-32)\\cdot {v}^{20}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499348 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1069 "q-93289" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1071 "The following table shows Herbert's height and the length of his shadow, in centimeters. \n\n| Height | Shadow |\n| --- | --- |\n| $197$ | $162$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the height of the object to the length of its ​shadow is the same for different objects at the same moment of time. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the length of the shadow cast by Regina at the same time, if her height is $150$ centimeters." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1072 #uuid "e3b2f10a-1224-55b9-ac80-e23ac5f6c46a" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499550 1074 "First, let's round Herbert's height and the length of his shadow:\n\n- Height: $197⟶200$\n\n- Shadow: $162⟶160$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the height to the length of the ​shadow (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{200}{160}.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the height and the denominator represents the length of the ​shadow.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{200÷10}{160÷10}=\\frac{20}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20÷4}{16÷4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a numerator of $150$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $30$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 30}{4\\times 30}=\\frac{150}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the shadow cast by Regina is approximately $120$ centimeters." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499331 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1069 "q-60067" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1071 "The number of roses and orchids in Margaret's garden is shown in the following table:\n\n| Roses | Orchids |\n| --- | --- |\n| $33$ | $18$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn Serena's garden, there are approximately $30$ orchids. The ratio of the number of roses to the number of orchids is equal in both gardens. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the number of roses in Serena's garden." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1072 #uuid "e57d984e-c871-52fc-b8d7-34ff3957d67c" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499551 1074 "First, let's round the number of roses and orchids in Margaret's garden:\n\n- Roses: $33⟶30$\n\n- Orchids: $18⟶20$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of roses to the number of orchids (expressed as a fraction) is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{20}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the number of roses and the denominator represents the number of orchids.\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$ gives an equivalent fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30÷10}{20÷10}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $30$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $15$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 15}{2\\times 15}=\\frac{45}{30}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $45$ roses in Serena's garden." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499350 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1069 "q-142989" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1071 "The weight of metal sheets produced by a particular machine is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.$ During a quality check, $25$ metal sheets are sampled from the daily production, and it is found that their mean weight is $2.3\\,\\text{kg}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire production. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1072 #uuid "e6c6accb-373d-5aa8-b43f-81ebbf038603" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499552 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=2.3\\,\\text{kg},\\,n=25,\\,\\sigma =0.4\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{0.4}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 2.3\\pm 0.206\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499388 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1069 "q-142496" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1071 "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=81$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =6.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-15,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1072 #uuid "e735b6cb-9b8a-5d37-9b58-b848a68baaea" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499553 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ -15\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499319 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1069 "q-72389" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1071 "Consider the plane curve $2{e}^{xy}=x+y+1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1072 #uuid "ea587ab0-d077-5468-b262-1697c88c7094" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499554 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{xy} & =x+y+1 \\\\ 2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,1)={f}_{y}(0,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(0,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{e}^{xy}-1)\\,i-(2y{e}^{xy}-1)\\,j \\\\ t(0,1) & =(2(0){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,i-(2(1){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i-j.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499316 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499332 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499404 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1069 "q-556457" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1071 "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$ Write these three numbers in increasing order.\n\nThe three numbers in increasing order are\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1072 #uuid "eabdbec5-6ee0-5977-9aec-0268ef8c060f" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499555 1074 "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect powers just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$\nSince $8<15<27,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{8}<\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt[3]{27}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt[3]{15}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{50}:$\nSince $27<50<64,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{27}<\\sqrt[3]{50}<\\sqrt[3]{64}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is the greatest of the three numbers because it is greater than $3,$ while the other two are less than $3.$ \n\nHowever, both $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$ are between $2$ and $3.$ To compare them, we need to test some decimal values. We can square or cube decimals to find closer approximations:\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$ \nSince ${2.4}^{3}=13.824$ and ${2.5}^{3}=15.625,$ we know that $2.4<\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5.$\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince ${2.6}^{2}=6.76$ and ${2.7}^{2}=7.29,$ we know that $2.6<\\sqrt{7}<2.7.$\n\nComparing these tighter bounds, we see that $\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5<2.6<\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nCombining all our comparisons, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt[3]{50}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers in increasing order are $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499414 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1069 "q-556444" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{85}$ lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1072 #uuid "eee5e7a8-3d1e-565b-891d-6f10ecae6e7f" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499556 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{85}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $85$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{85}$ lies.\nWe know that ${9}^{2}=81$ and ${10}^{2}=100$.\nSince $81<85<100$, we know that $9<\\sqrt{85}<10$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $9$ and $10$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.1}^{2} & =82.81 \\\\ {9.2}^{2} & =84.64 \\\\ {9.3}^{2} & =86.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $84.64<85<86.49$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{9.2}^{2}<85<{9.3}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n9.2<\\sqrt{85}<9.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{85}$ lies between $9.2$ and $9.3$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499413 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1069 "q-87759" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1071 "![](images/56/56c2afca682997fa2ead3f4962d5de4e347d99164e257737f68ddafea385ae4f.png)\n\nWhat is a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1072 #uuid "ef93482b-ea08-53c2-acb8-85ef03af9f1c" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499557 1074 "We have four pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R & ≅∠F \\\\ ∠S & ≅∠E \\\\ ∠T & ≅∠H \\\\ ∠U & ≅∠G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have four pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =FE \\\\ ST & =EH \\\\ TU & =HG \\\\ UR & =GF.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the two quadrilaterals are congruent. In particular\n\n- vertex $R$ corresponds to vertex $F,$\n\n- vertex $S$ corresponds to vertex $E,$\n\n- vertex $T$ corresponds to vertex $H,$ and\n\n- vertex $U$ corresponds to vertex $G.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nRSTU≅FEHG.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499338 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1069 "q-345151" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1071 "![](images/72/725398d9e534fbc888ef9963c9075538aa70cde9317c664b253394fb63cff052.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1072 #uuid "f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499558 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta =\\frac{16}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{72}{125})}^{2}+{(\\frac{24}{125})}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499359 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1069 "q-60032" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1071 "The following table shows the number of days it rained in Madrid during $2019.$\n\n| Rainy days | No-rain days |\n| --- | --- |\n| $63$ | $302$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, determine the approximate ratio of the number of days it rained in Madrid to the number of days it did not rain." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1072 #uuid "f3715793-c326-5ff1-9566-caa835ab04e3" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499559 1074 "First, let's approximate the number of days that it rained and did not rain by rounding to the nearest $10$:\n\n- Rainy days: $63⟶60$\n\n- No-rain days: $302⟶300$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of days that it rained to the number of days that it did not rain is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{300}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{60÷10}{300÷10}=\\frac{6}{30}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6÷6}{30÷6}=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nHence, the ratio of the number of days that it rained and did not rain is approximately $1:5.$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499422 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1069 "q-345129" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1071 "![](images/f5/f5c38b340f0e64375d0e163bddaf194cba4bf11f4ef123d3a717fac72e2da94f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1072 #uuid "f5080c7a-f6e0-5a11-a002-8f26aa8f47df" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499560 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n5(0)+20(0)+5q=10,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n5q=10\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $2$ coulombs." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499381 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1069 "q-143517" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3.6$ and $4.2.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7580\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7611\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7642\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7881\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7910\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7939\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8159\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8186\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8212\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1072 #uuid "f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499561 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{8}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499370 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1069 "q-345098" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $60\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1072 #uuid "f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499562 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/e1/e13f3dd8d881385444b215f62e0c43d6eb40d36e4b6af5f4792cba0502eb2ea2.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499321 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1069 "q-345057" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1072 #uuid "f79f0727-9270-5c79-bad1-22894e26808b" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499563 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb44fa46fed9427723f2ff5f6fd31011271f411d5dbc58e232bc5b8bbd86607.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n10=32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(10) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =32\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0.\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499368 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1069 "q-162567" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1071 "![](images/9f/9f2f77c6c2047dcd3f542fc0f640a68b45a4d40784d0e7eaacdcadb002c79de5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1072 #uuid "faed1d58-d701-5e29-bb64-3a9beae53a8b" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499564 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nOf the given options, the only level curves tangential to the vector field at every point are shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd47775ffb4cba4cc01dde0bf404dca12bdeecc24e2f58639c04f1feda8293ee.png)" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499340 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1069 "q-556449" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lie?" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1072 #uuid "fbc69846-f4f2-5c08-841b-d6a9c38b8533" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499565 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $150$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${6}^{3}=216$.\nSince $125<150<216$, we know that $5<\\sqrt[3]{150}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{3} & =140.608 \\\\ {5.3}^{3} & =148.877 \\\\ {5.4}^{3} & =157.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $148.877<150<157.464$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{3}<150<{5.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.3<\\sqrt[3]{150}<5.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies between $5.3$ and $5.4$." 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499372 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1069 "q-143501" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1072 #uuid "fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499566 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499567 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499579 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499567 1062 #uuid "01b09de2-5016-5421-a764-c1a1b5c31868" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499567 1063 #edb/ref 17592186278518 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499568 1062 #uuid "01bfa9b8-ea54-5e15-adea-d773f4a9fc72" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499568 1063 #edb/ref 17592186371913 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499569 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499600 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499569 1062 #uuid "05a2099a-d88b-5b18-b5ca-d033c5db5614" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499569 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124970 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499570 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499585 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499570 1062 #uuid "085a5662-c83a-5261-aadd-4e17a1724de2" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499570 1063 #edb/ref 17592186278495 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499571 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499578 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499571 1062 #uuid "0aa5fab4-668b-577d-91a3-ac72b6d14200" 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499571 1063 #edb/ref 17592186373339 13194139535343 17592186045417 true] [17592186499572 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499594 131941395353